; errors.loon — Error handling guide
[fn guide-errors-page []
  [doc-page
    "Error Handling"
    "Option, Result, and the ? operator."
    
    [doc-section
      "Option Type"
      [doc-text
        "Loon has no "
        [doc-code "nil"]
        " and no "
        [doc-code "null"]
        ". When a value might not exist, the type system says so out loud through "
        [doc-code "Option"]
        ". A null pointer exception can never reach you at runtime, "
        "because the compiler will not let the absent case go unhandled."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[let found [get {:a 1} :b]]\n"
          "; found is None\n"
          "\n"
          "[let found [get {:a 1} :a]]\n"
          "; found is [Some 1]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Wrap a present value in "
        [doc-code "Some"]
        ", and reach for "
        [doc-code "None"]
        " when there is nothing to wrap. Looking up a missing key in a map "
        "hands you "
        [doc-code "None"]
        ", not a silent null waiting to detonate later."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Result Type"
      [doc-text
        "When an operation can fail, Loon carries the outcome in a "
        [doc-code "Result"]
        ". The approach echoes Rust's, and for the same reason: "
        "failure becomes explicit, with none of the action-at-a-distance of exceptions."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[fn parse-int [s]\n"
          "  [match [try-parse s]\n"
          "    [Some n] [Ok n]\n"
          "    None [Err \"not a number\"]]]"]]
      [doc-text
        [doc-code "Ok"]
        " wraps a success; "
        [doc-code "Err"]
        " wraps a failure. The caller is never caught off guard, "
        "because the return type announces that this function can fail."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Pattern Matching on Results"
      [doc-text
        "The most direct way to handle a "
        [doc-code "Result"]
        " is "
        [doc-code "match"]
        ". You spell out both outcomes, "
        "and the compiler makes sure neither slips through."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[match [parse-int \"42\"]\n"
          "  [Ok n] [println [str \"got: \" n]]\n"
          "  [Err e] [println [str \"error: \" e]]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "The verbosity is the point. Matching on a Result is you "
        "deciding, in the open, what failure means here. "
        "The compiler will not let you wave it away."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "The ? Operator"
      [doc-text
        "Matching by hand wears thin when a chain of operations can each fail in turn. "
        "The "
        [doc-code "?"]
        " operator is Loon's relief: "
        "it unwraps an "
        [doc-code "Ok"]
        " in place and hands any "
        [doc-code "Err"]
        " straight back to the caller."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[fn load-config [path]\n"
          "  [let text [IO.read-file path]?]\n"
          "  [let data [parse-json text]?]\n"
          "  [Ok data]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "If "
        [doc-code "IO.read-file"]
        " returns an "
        [doc-code "Err"]
        ", the function returns that error at once and the rest of the body never runs. "
        "If it returns "
        [doc-code "Ok"]
        ", the inner value binds to "
        [doc-code "text"]
        " and execution flows on. You get the brevity of exceptions "
        "with the candor of Result types."]
      [doc-note
        "info"
        "The ? operator works on both Result and Option. On None it returns None early."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Try Blocks"
      [doc-text
        "Sometimes you want "
        [doc-code "?"]
        " inside a block, "
        "but you want to catch the error here rather than send it upward. "
        "That is the job of "
        [doc-code "try"]
        ". It wraps a block of code "
        "and returns a Result."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[let result [try\n"
          "  [let a [parse-int \"10\"]?]\n"
          "  [let b [parse-int \"bad\"]?]\n"
          "  [+ a b]]]\n"
          "; result is [Err \"not a number\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Inside a "
        [doc-code "try"]
        " block you may use "
        [doc-code "?"]
        " without restraint. Let any operation fail and the whole block evaluates to that error; "
        "let them all succeed and you get "
        [doc-code "Ok"]
        " carrying the block's final value."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Handle for Effects"
      [doc-text
        "For errors that must travel across handler boundaries, "
        "Loon offers the "
        [doc-code "Fail"]
        " effect, the bridge "
        "between effect-based code and error handling."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[handle [divide 10 0]\n"
          "  [Fail.raise msg] [println [str \"caught: \" msg]]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "The handler catches the failure and chooses its fate: "
        "log it, return a default, retry, or pass it further along."]
      [doc-note
        "tip"
        "Prefer Result and ? for local error handling. Use Fail for errors that need to cross handler boundaries."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Error Codes"
      [doc-text
        "Every compiler error arrives with an alphanumeric code. "
        "When the message alone leaves you guessing, run "
        [doc-code "loon explain"]
        " with the code for the full account."]
      [code-block
        {:label "SHELL"}
        [str
          "$ loon explain E0042\n"
          "E0042: Use after move\n"
          "\n"
          "A value was used after its ownership\n"
          "was transferred to another binding.\n"
          "..."]]
      [doc-text
        "Each explanation gives the rule at work, a minimal example "
        "that trips it, and a fix to try. They reward reading, "
        "above all while the ownership system is still new to you."]]
    
    [doc-prev-next "Testing" "/guide/testing" "Syntax" "/ref/syntax"]]]
